These studies focused on the importance of coolant fluid state change and HTD for hydrogen and methane; however, none of them provides further insight on the HTD conditions. In contrast, various CFD methods have been developed to simulate the thermal behavior of the coolant flows at supercritical pressure [6,9,14–23]. Although these methods generally predict the pressure drop and flow properties with acceptable accuracy, they are less precise in estimating the wall temperature that is very important for the thermal analysis of the cooling channels. Moreover, few of these studies [14–20] were devoted to investigating the thermal behavior of transcritical coolant fluids under high heat fluxes and the resulting HTD. In the following, some of the most relevant studies are reviewed. Wang et al. [14] conducted a comprehensive numerical study on the heat transfer of the methane at supercritical pressure flowing into a horizontal mini-tube and investigated the effect of flow parameters on the Nusselt number variations. They proposed a modified heat transfer expression for the supercritical methane. Further, they showed that the local HTD can occur in the transcritical region under a high heat flux due to the significant wall temperature increment. Urbano and Nasuti [15,16] performed a parametric study on the methane flow at supercritical pressure in an axisymmetric straight heated tube. They discussed the effect of different flow variables such as coolant inlet temperature, pressure, and mass flow rate on the HTD onset. They depicted that the critical wall heat flux value that constitutes an indication of the HTD onset, is linearly proportional to the coolant inlet pressure at
