the transcritical coolant and heat transfer is required. In addition, to the best of authors’ knowledge, variations of the transport properties have not been investigated accurately along
the cooling channel at near-critical conditions and deterioration of heat transfer. In this article, the equations of state and the transport relations are presented for estimation of the thermo-physical properties of transcritical methane and hydrogen. A finite-volume based computational fluid dynamic (CFD) solver is employed to solve the governing equations. The thermal behavior of the methane coolant flow in straight and curved rectangular channels under asymmetric heating and hydrogen in a straight tube was simulated in transcritical condition. The experimental data of Ref. [11] and numerical results of Ref. [20] were used to evaluate the utilized turbulent models and thermo-fluidic relations, respectively. The main contribution of this paper is to simulate and study the HTD in transcritical methane flow as well as the fluid state change inside a straight/curved rectangular cooling channel utilizing reliable turbulence models and thermo-fluidic relations. In addition, variations of the transport properties and density distribution of the coolant flow are proposed as criteria for the recognition of the HTD and transcritical regions, respectively. Moreover, the effects of mass flow rate, outlet pressure, wall temperature, surface roughness, and the channel geometry on the thermal behavior of the coolant fluid (i.e. HTD and fluid state change) are investigated.
2. Thermo-fluidic properties
Methane enters the cooling channel at supercritical pressure and subcritical temperature and exits the channel at supercritical temperature due to the heat absorption. Critical pressure, temperature, and density for methane are 45.9 bar, 190.5 K, and 162.7 kg
m3 ,
respectively. In such cases, coolant fluid undergoes a pseudo-phase change from a liquid-like state to a gas-like state by passing over the critical temperature. Further, the thermodynamic parameters (e.g. isobaric specific heat and sound speed) experience serious changes in the near-critical region [19]. Hence, the main step in analyzing the coolant thermo-fluidic behavior in transcritical condition is the investigation of the equations of state and transport


