4. Numerical treatment
The present computations are performed with a finite-volume pressure-based CFD solver. Couple algorithm is used to match pressure and velocity fields. Spatial discretization was preceded using a least squares cell-based algorithm for gradients. The bounded central differencing scheme for momentum and energy is used. Moreover, the second order upwind scheme is adopted for both turbulent kinetic energy and specific dissipation rate in the case of K –ω models, as well as for turbulent viscosity in the case of S–A model. For the time discretization, the first order implicit scheme is used. The local criterion for numerical convergence (i.e. the maximum relative difference between two consecutive iterations), is less than 10−12 for the energy equation. Moreover, direct use of the state equation and the transport property relations for calculation of fluid properties (i.e. ρ, μ, k, and C p ) increases the total computational runtime considerably [40]. Therefore, the values of thermodynamic properties are tabulated and inserted into the CFD solver via linear interpolation. Hence, the fluid properties can be calculated at each computational cell with much less computational effort.
5. Numerical test cases and boundary conditions
5.1. Test Case 1: hydrogen in a heated tube
The experimental data of Ref. [11] on hydrogen as the coolant in a heated tube at transcritical condition is utilized as the validation case for the present numerical simulation. Accordingly, the test case “0681” from [11] is adopted to validate the CFD solution. As shown in Fig. 2, an unheated length (L1) is considered in order to assure a fully-developed flow field at the entrance of the main tube (L2). Then, a non-uniform heat flux distribution is imposed to the hydrogen along L2. The geometrical parameters and the boundary conditions of the selected test case are sketched in Fig. 3. Here, the mass flow rate is Q = 0.08 kg/s, the total temperature of the inlet is set to be T0 = 25.056 K, the static pressure of the outlet is Pe = 1.613 MPa and the no-slip condition is applied over the walls. The hydraulic diameter of the tube is 8.5 mm. The hydrogen flow within the tube is assumed to be axisymmetric and steady. The pressure of the coolant fluid inside the channel is at supercritical condition while the temperature varies from a subcritical state to a supercritical state; hence, the system is operating


