5.2. Test Case 2: methane in high aspect ratio rectangular channels
Transcritical methane flow is simulated in three-dimensional high aspect ratio 0.1 × 0.8 × 30 mm straight and curved rectangular channels. The geometry presented in Refs. [21] and [23] is adopted for the straight and curved channels respectively. The boundary conditions of Ref. [21] are used for both cases. Geometry, boundary conditions, and a schematic view of the computational mesh of these channels are shown in Fig. 5. Further, because of the symmetrical geometry and side wall boundary conditions, the coolant flow is simulated in one half of the channel. The total pressure and temperature of the inlet are set to be P0 = 9 MPa and T0 = 130 K, the static pressure of the outlet is Pe = 7 MPa, the temperature of the top and bottom walls are Tb = 600 K and Tt = 130 K, and a linear distribution of temperature is applied to the side wall. Also, the no-slip condition is imposed on all walls. The hydraulic diameter of the channel is 0.17 mm. According to Table 2 and Fig. 6, various computational grids with different resolutions are tested for the mesh independence study and the fine grid with the size of 32 × 92 × 100 (base × height × stream-wise) and y+ less than 1.0 is selected as the optimum mesh.
