models (i.e. S–A, K –ω, and SST K –ω) were evaluated. The wall temperature distribution resulted from utilizing these turbulence models are compared to the experimental data of Ref. [11] in Fig. 7. The accuracy of each turbulence model in respect to this experimental data is reported in Table 3 in terms of absolute average deviation (AAD). Fig. 7 shows that the K –ω model has more deviations from the experimental data in some areas of the cooling channel. This is due to the model sensitivity to the values of ω at the inlet for the boundary layer computations in the internal flows [37]. This deficiency has been removed in the SST K –ω model by considering cross-diffusion source terms in the ω transport equation [38]. This model uses the Standard K –ω in near-wall regions and implements the Standard K –ε outside the boundary layer [39]. Therefore, the SST K –ω model generally has higher accuracy near the wall while being less sensitive to the flow outside the boundary layer. The results of Fig. 7 and Table 3 evidence that the SST K –ω model offers the highest accuracy and shows the best compatibility with the experimental data. This model is also more accurate in calculating the turbulent viscosity due to the correction of production terms in the turbulent kinetic energy equation and utilization of the transport equation for the turbulent shear stress. On the contrary, the S–A model takes advantage of a transport equation for the turbulent viscosity and is very dependent on the grid size in the near wall region. Consequently, the SST K –ω model is exploited in the present study to capture the thermo-fluid dynamic of the transcritical coolant flow, although, the S–A model can be used for approximate solutions and the general studies of the coolant flows. The numerical results of Pizzarelli et al. [20] are used to investigate the effects of implemented state equation and transport property relations on the simulation of transcritical hydrogen flow for the Test Case 1. P