6.1.2. Mass flow rate effect
In order to explore the region of coolant fluid state change (transcritical regime) and HTD phenomenon, the variations of the heat transfer coefficient, wall temperature, near-wall isobaric specific heat, thermal conductivity, and viscosity along the tube under the boundary conditions of Test Case 1 ( Q = 0.08 kg/s) are illustrated in Fig. 8. The region in which C p experiences a sharp change (the maximum value of C p is about Z = 340 mm) indicates the location of the transcritical regime [16,19]. In this region the coolant state changes from liquid-like to gas-like. Generally, HTD is characterized by a severe reduction in the convective heat transfer coefficient and a peak in the wall temperature along the channel. Therefore, wall temperature (T w ), heat convection coefficient (h), and the wall heat flux (q) distributions are key parameters in HTD region recognition [15,20,21]. In the rest of this paper, T w , h, k, and μ are used for determination of HTD region in Test Case 1 and q, h, k, and μ are adopted for Test Case 2. Fig. 8 shows that there are areas where the wall temperature encounters with the sharp local increment (i.e. the wall temperature increases from about 70 K to 290 K in the range of Z = 420 to 590 mm). Simultaneously, in this area, the thermal conductivity and viscosity reveal an increasing trend. This area depicts the HTD region in which the heat transfer coefficient decreases dramatically. Further, the region of fluid state change and HTD are relatively overlapped. To study the effect of mass flow rate changes on the location of transcritical regime along the tube, the isobaric specific heat (C p ) distribution is studied in different mass flow rates. Fig. 9 shows the near-wall isobaric specific heat distribution for different mass flow rates from 0.08 to 0.11 kg/s. Also, the location of maximum C p ( ZC p,max ) is given in the figure legend. Increasing the mass flow rate results in an increment in ZC p,max that postpones the transcritical region and extends it along the tube. To consider the effect of mass flow rate changes on the HTD location, the variations of the wall temperature, near-wall thermal conductivity, and viscosity along the tube for different mass flow rates are illustrated in Figs. 10–12, respectively. By increasing mass flow rate, HTD is delayed with a lower intensity.
