6.3. Methane in the curved rectangular cooling channel
Methane is simulated in the curved rectangular channel (that is the case of Vulcain engine [23] in the vicinity of the throat), to study the effect of channel curvature on the thermal behavior of the transcritical coolant. Fig. 23 displays the contours of several flow parameters in both straight and curved channels at different cross-sections. In this figure, the right side of each cross section stands for the straight channel results, as the left side is for the curved channel. The results (Figs. 23a, and 23b) reveal that the average density decreases and the velocity magnitude increases along the channel as the gas layer emerges. The average density at the outlet of the curved channel is 1.3% higher than the straight channel, and velocity is 4% less. The curvature results in a thinner gas layer at the second half part of the channel. The results for μ, k, and temperature reveal to be less sensitive to the channel curvature as depicted in Figs. 23c, 23d, and 23e. Further, Fig. 23f shows that the average value of C p increases along the channel as the coolant fluid absorbs heat. This value for the curved channel is about 12% more than the straight one at the outlet. The curvature leads to more extended C p variations and fluid state change region at the second half part of the channel. Fig. 24 depicts the isobaric specific heat and viscosity on the symmetry plane at the outlet of the curved and straight channels to examine the effect of channel curvature on the region of HTD and fluid state change. As seen from the figure, the HTD occurs at a lower height in the curved channel at a certain cross-section and the fluid state change region is more extended as explained before.
7. Conclusions
A survey of the literature indicated that most previous numerical studies have focused on the HTD of the transcritical methane flow inside circular cooling channels. Whereas, the actual engine cooling channels are typically rectangular, curved, and heated asymmetrically. In addition, variations of the transport properties have not been investigated accurately along the cooling channel at near-critical conditions and deterioration of heat transfer. The purpose of the current article was to comprehensively study the thermal behavior, HTD, and fluid state change of the transcritical hydrogen and methane flow under the effect of different parame
ters inside a heated tube and straight/curved rectangular channels with asymmetric heating. The real gas state equations, the transport property relations, and turbulence models were examined for proper simulations. Three-dimensional analyses for the turbulent flow of the transcritical coolant and heat transfer were conducted. The accuracy of the present numerical approach was validated with numerical and experimental data. The main findings of the present paper are:
1. The region in which C p experiences a sharp change, indicating the transcritical regime, is located in the vicinity of the inflection point of the density curve. 2. The change of thermal conductivity and viscosity curve slope is proposed as a criterion for locating the HTD region in which the wall temperature shows a local sharp increment. 3. Increasing the mass flow rate of the transcritical hydrogen inside a heated tube shifts the maximum value of C p to farther downstream, which postpones and elongates the transcritical region along the tube. This also results in a delayed and less intensified HTD. 4. Increasing the bottom wall temperature of the straight rectangular channel thickens the gas-layer and also increases the velocity magnitude and wall heat flux of the transcritical methane. Moreover, the fluid state change and HTD region occur in a higher position. 5. The reduction of outlet pressure of methane flow leads to a velocity magnitude and heat flux increment near the bottom wall. Further, the location of a change in the conductivity diagram slope, which is corresponding to the HTD region, shows to be independent of the outlet pressure. The value of isobaric specific heat peak was increased with decreasing outlet pressure; while, the fluid state change region and the inflection point of density curve showed infinitesimal variations. 6. Surface roughness affects the local maximum heat flux due to the thermal equilibrium improvement. However, it has a negligible effect on both HTD region and the methane state change location. 7. The curvature of the channel has a direct effect on the gas layer thickness and C p at the second half of the channel. Further, the HTD occurs at a lower height and the fluid state change region is more extended.
